==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{q|South Asia only}} {{alt|en|nala|nalla|nallah|nulla}}

===Etymology===
Borrowed from {{bor|en|bn|নালা}}, {{bor|en|hi|नाला}}, from {{der|en|sa|नाडी}}.

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|chiefly|South Asia}} A [[stream]]-bed, [[ravine]], or other [[watercourse]]; a [[drain]] for rain or floodwater. {{defdate|from 17th c.}}
#* '''1849''', Brigadier Lockwood, Report of 2nd Cavalry Division at Battle of Goojerat
#*: About this time a large [[gole]] of horsemen came on towards me, and I proposed to charge; but as they turned at once from the fire of the guns, and as there was a '''nullah''' in front, I refrained from advancing after them.
#* '''1924''', {{w|EM Forster}}, ''A Passage to India'', Penguin 2005, p. 82:
#*: It was just after the exit from a bridge; the animal had probably come up out of the '''nullah'''.
# {{lb|en|Hong Kong}} An [[open-air]], [[concrete]]-lined [[channel]] for [[drain]]ing rain or wastewater.

